verdict.evidence:
Location: src/haute/routes/files.py:110-125 (JSONL row-count estimator). Two claims. (1) `max(n_preview, 1)` is redundant — PROVABLY sound: `n_preview = len(preview_df)` is the frame height; the enclosing `if n_preview > 0` guarantees `n_preview >= 1`, so `max(n_preview, 1) == n_preview` exactly. (2) Line 114 (`avg_line_bytes = file_size / max(n_preview, 1)`) is dead because `if sample_bytes > 0` (line 120) always holds when `n_preview > 0` — correct for all reachable inputs, verified empirically on polars 1.39.2: a zero-column frame is forced to height 0 (`pl.DataFrame({'x':[1,2]}).select([])` yields height=0, ndjson=''), so 'rows but 0 columns' is NOT constructible and `n_preview>0` implies >=1 column; every row with >=1 column serialises to a non-empty `{...}
` line (smallest observed `{"a":""}
` = 9 bytes; `{"a":null}
` = 11), and splitlines() yields exactly `n_preview` non-empty elements with no blank trailing line. Hence `sample_bytes >= n_preview >= 1 > 0` whenever the guard is entered, line 121 always fires, and line 114's value is always discarded. Behavior-preserving on every reachable input: TRUE. Caveats placing this at marginal not sound: (a) benefit is tiny (~2 net lines for a function-local micro-cleanup); (b) the removed `if sample_bytes > 0` guard currently prevents a ZeroDivisionError at line 122 (`file_size / avg_line_bytes`), and the deadness argument relies on a NON-LOCAL invariant about Polars `write_ndjson` formatting (an external lib whose serialization could change across versions); (c) existing tests (tests/test_files_routes.py:232 happy path asserts only row_count>0; :401 empty-file asserts row_count==0) do NOT pin the `sample_bytes==0` boundary, so a future regression reintroducing div-by-zero would go uncaught. Per project CLAUDE.md guidance on not removing loud-failure guards, this is a judgement-call cleanup rather than an obvious win. No null/empty/ordering/side-effect case breaks today.
